================================================================================
2026-09-01  The published ODAF fleet article was obtained from its author and read
================================================================================
Context:
  - L. Paulhiac and R. Desquiens, "Dynamic Thermal Model for Oil Directed Air Forced
    Power Transformers With Cooling Stage Representation," IEEE Trans. Power Del.,
    vol. 37, no. 5, pp. 4135-4144, Oct. 2022, doi 10.1109/TPWRD.2022.3145003.
    Sent by an author. The copy is licence-stamped, stays untracked, and is not
    redistributed. The five-week open item "obtain the article" is closed.
Findings that cost us something:
  - That article's Table IV names the three units of the source dataset. It publishes
    fitted parameters and out-of-sample RMSE for the same transformers used in the
    private field-validation work. It is direct prior art, not adjacent work.
  - Its stated applications include cooling-efficiency monitoring and load-capability
    evaluation, with a measured cleaning case. No novelty is claimed here for either.
    A prior internal note calling fouling detection an opportunity was withdrawn.
Findings that help:
  - Loss ratio for the 360 MVA unit sourced at R = 612/90 = 6.8, replacing an assumed
    6.0. The same fit then gives 1.55 K out-of-sample hot-spot RMSE, against 1.54 K.
    The number recorded is the measured one; it rests on a sourced constant now.
  - That model treats the winding time constant as a required input, not a fitted
    parameter, which is the convention already used here.
  - Its own first cooling stage on that unit falls outside its theoretical assumptions
    and is handled by an empirical special case.
  - It reports no confidence intervals, no identifiability analysis, no information
    bound and no refusal criterion. The identifiability work remains the differentiator,
    now checked against the nearest paper rather than assumed.
Hypothesis tested and FALSIFIED:
  - private/viscosity_exponent.py asked whether that article's oil-viscosity correction
    explains the ONAF apparent-exponent drift recorded here. Two falsifiers were stated
    before the run and both fired: the coefficient lands at 0.55-0.70 against a
    published 0.25-0.45, and held-out error roughly doubles (RMSE 4.41 -> 7.92 K).
    Decisively, the implied apparent exponent FALLS with load where the measurement
    RISES. Viscosity is not the mechanism. The buoyancy reading is unaffected and the
    load-dependent exponent stays empirical.
  - This is why the reply to that author was rewritten before sending. An earlier draft
    asserted the opposite.
Code:
  - staged.py: CLEAN_COOLER_PRECONDITION, stated on every staged report. Fouling is
    undetectable from load, ambient, top-oil or stage label, and inflates the rated oil
    rise; parameters identified clean and applied fouled under-predict the hot spot.
  - staged.py, crlb.py: a refusal now says it is a statement about THIS model form, not
    about the record. A near-constant load starves the exponentiated IEC terms, while a
    model parameterised on cooling-plant fraction and oil viscosity can be identifiable
    on the same data. The old wording overclaimed.
Documentation:
  - ASSESSMENT.md: the prior-art gap is now a directed check of the nearest paper. The
    "no one else is stating this" commissioning claim is qualified -- that article
    proposes a designed acceptance-test profile; what remains unstated is the criterion.
  - EVIDENCE.md: claims 99-105 added; claims 58 and 61 annotated, not rewritten.
  - paper/main.tex: related work now engages the article instead of talking around it.
Verification:
